<!-- The ground behind the crescent.

     No FLAT colour can serve this artwork, and that is the whole reason this
     file exists. The foreground spans tone 27.7 at the night horn to 73.4 at
     the dawn horn, so any single value contrasts with one end and swallows the
     other. The shipped icon was #12161B: it read the dawn horn at 8.79:1 and
     the night horn at 1.79:1, which is to say it read as a black tile with an
     orange fingernail on it, and was reported as the darkest icon on the
     launcher it sat in. It was stale as well - #12161B was Dusk's surface
     before the 31 Aug 2026 palette rebuild and was never updated with it.

     TWO things were measured rather than reasoned about, and both first
     attempts were wrong.

     THE AXIS. Read off the RENDERED icon, not the path data: the cool half of
     the artwork centres at x=0.35 of the tile and the warm half at x=0.63, so
     the split is very nearly horizontal. A first attempt assumed 45 degrees
     from the crescent's -32 degree rotation and put the light in the wrong
     corner. Hence startY 68 to endY 40 - left to right with only a slight rise.

     THE RAMP stays inside ONE hue family. A warm-light to cool-dark gradient
     loses its chroma in the middle - measured at 7, against the 18 a single-hue
     ramp holds - and the icon came out grey-brown through its centre while both
     of its ends were fine. That midpoint is the thing to check when changing
     these stops.

     Light on the left behind the cool horn (9.0:1), deep on the right behind
     the warm one (2.61:1). THAT SECOND NUMBER IS BELOW 3:1 AND IS DELIBERATE.
     It was not arrived at by not looking: the step that clears the guideline
     (tone 41, 3.01:1) was built, installed and compared against this one on the
     device, and this was chosen with the shortfall on the table. The reasons it
     holds are that the dawn horn is a ~40dp shape rather than a hairline or a
     glyph, and that it separates from this ground by HUE as much as by
     luminance - orange on blue - which a WCAG ratio does not model at all. 3:1
     is a floor written for small UI parts whose only cue is lightness.
     Do not read this as licence to go below 3:1 elsewhere, and do not "fix" it
     upward without looking at the two side by side first.
     The asymmetry is the practical note: the LEFT end has 9.0:1 where it needs
     3, so if this ever wants to be lighter again, lighten the LEFT and leave
     the right alone. Push the right end further and the orange horn really does
     start dissolving into its own background.

     ASKED TO BE LIGHTER AGAIN, AND THE LEFT STOP HAD NOTHING LEFT TO GIVE - it
     is tone 96.2. So the lift went into a THIRD stop at offset 0.55, tone ~85,
     which lightens the two thirds of the tile the eye reads as "the colour of
     the icon" while leaving offset 1.00 exactly where the dawn horn needs it.
     Moving the right stop was built and compared: tone 50 takes the horn from
     2.61:1 to 2.19:1, and there is no version of a LIGHT ground under a light
     horn that works - which is the whole reason the ramp runs diagonally in the
     first place.
     One measurement here is worth more than the change. Sampled off the render,
     the worst warm-artwork-versus-ground pair is 1.00:1 - IN THE SHIPPED ICON
     AS WELL as in every lighter variant. Orange and blue-grey at equal
     luminance. So no contrast ratio can referee this artwork at any stop value;
     it has always been carried by hue, and the 2.61:1 quoted above describes
     one pair rather than the worst one. Compare these on a launcher, not in a
     spreadsheet. -->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:aapt="http://schemas.android.com/aapt"
    android:width="108dp" android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108" android:viewportHeight="108">
    <path android:pathData="M0,0 h108 v108 h-108 Z">
        <aapt:attr name="android:fillColor">
            <gradient android:type="linear"
                android:startX="0" android:startY="68"
                android:endX="108" android:endY="40">
                    <item android:offset="0.00" android:color="#FFF0F4FF" />
                    <item android:offset="0.55" android:color="#FFCBD5E6" />
                    <item android:offset="1.00" android:color="#FF5A6B89" />
            </gradient>
        </aapt:attr>
    </path>
</vector>
